   | pop  (_ : Dst w)
   | setcc (_ : CondCode) (_ : Dst w) -- {_ : w = .W8}
   | cmovcc (_ : CondCode) (_ : Reg w) (src : RegOrMem w)
diff --git a/notes/x64-stos-greater.md b/notes/x64-stos-greater.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ec03a37
--- /dev/null
+++ b/notes/x64-stos-greater.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Sequential stores and signed greater condition
+
+The initialized random-provider refill reaches REP STOS in its stack cleanup
+and JG in ChaCha's round loop. STOS supports all four operand widths with
+64-bit address size. Each element requests ordinary write permission; DF
+controls the direction, REP consumes RCX, and count zero accesses no memory.
+Arithmetic and system flags are unchanged. Other address sizes fail explicitly.
+As elsewhere in this sequential model, interruptible restart and exception
+delivery are not represented. This extension does not justify those behaviors.
+
+The signed greater condition is ZF clear and SF equal to OF. Both JG/JNLE
+spellings use it, as do the existing generic SETcc and CMOVcc operations.
+
+Reference: Intel SDM, STOS and REP entries in
+[volume 2B](https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/public/us/en/documents/manuals/64-ia-32-architectures-software-developer-vol-2b-manual.pdf)
+and Jcc in
+[volume 2A](https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/public/us/en/documents/manuals/64-ia-32-architectures-software-developer-vol-2a-manual.pdf).
+
+`Kraken.X64.StosTests` checks widths, directions, single and repeated stores,
+zero count, unmapped memory, per-element permissions, rejected address modes,
+parsing and symbolic flag preservation. `Kraken.X64.GreaterTests` exhausts
+the three relevant flags, checks aliases and proves the branch effect.
+Build and replay both with the consumer's Lean 4.35.0 toolchain.
-- 
